  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1200 PM MST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nModels are still trending with the split flow regime centered over \nthe Intermountain West. Which means the chance for any precipitation \nis likely to fade quickly for the long term forecast. A weak area of \nhigh pressure will essentially wobble around the Intermountain West \nas Low pressure systems move across the southern United states and \nthe Canadian providences. Temperatures will increase slightly \nthroughout the work week. Tuesday temperatures will be a little \ncooler in the upper 40's with the temperature climbing to the mid \n50's by Thursday. These warmer temperatures don't come to us free of \ncharge though. Our ridge begins to break down slightly as a couple \nupper level lows begin to move across the Canadian Providences \nbefore diving through the Northern Plains by Friday. The first \nsystem begins to dive Wednesday afternoon increasing our cloud cover \nand start the increase of winds in the afternoon. This sets the \nstage for a potential high wind event starting late Thursday evening \nand eventually ending Friday night into early Saturday morning. The \nmajor cause of our high winds will be the second upper level low \ntransversing a little more south into Montana and the Dakotas \nplacing the jetstream right over Wyoming. Some models (AI-GFS) may \nbe overdoing the winds a touch but it gets the point across. \nThanksgiving will be generally warm for this time of year with \ntemperatures in the low 50's with the winds slowly increasing \nthroughout the evening as family wind down from the holiday \nfestivities. Thursday night into Friday morning the Low pressure \nsystem will make its presence known as it further compresses the \nheight gradient over the Southeast Wyoming region. The Craig to \nCasper gradient looks to jump from 31 meters to 73 meters around \n11PM Thursday to 1 AM Friday. Looking at the global omega fields, \nmaxed out subsident values followed by strong rising motion give \nsupport to the mountain wave/high wind ideology. Friday afternoon \nlooks to be the peak of the mountain wave intensity as the 700mb jet \nclimbs to about 65 to 70 knots. By Friday evening and into the \novernight hours the winds will gradually calm down to our normal \nWyoming breezy levels. The plus side will be that due to the \ndownsloping winds it will be a 50/50 day Friday(50 mph winds and \nafternoon temperatures in the 50's).",
